{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^#.*(D726|D756|D757|D758)|D726 —|D75[678] —'\"' docs/DECISIONS-log.md\"","aggregated_output":"10991:## D726 · 2026-11-22 · Alex opens a judge version for related number questions ($0 gate first) and agrees to \"Forecast unavailable\" as the first-release interim\n11799:## D755 · 2026-11-23 · The paid re-send STOPs (complete); council 71: close v13s at STOP and carry D726's \"Forecast unavailable\" into the first release\n11823:## D756 · 2026-11-23 · Alex closes the v13s line; the first release shows number questions as \"Forecast unavailable\"\n11842:## D757 · 2026-11-23 · Toward production: the \"Forecast unavailable\" release design is ready for when Alex opens release work (council 72–72d); two choices go to Alex\n11867:## D758 · 2026-11-23 · Under D439 the council opens local release work for \"Forecast unavailable\"; non-number questions show no explanation text (§4 a); the v13s checker defects are closed as won't-fix (council 73)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md | head -n 110; wc -l docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Core PRD for the next phase: filing must recognise indirect relations the way an analyst does\n     2\t\n     3\t> Status: in progress (opened 2026-11-04). Alex: \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\".\n     4\t> This page is the single source for this phase. In a conflict: Alex's words → `docs/DECISIONS.md` → this page → the other design documents. This page's\n     5\t> conclusions, evidence, constraints, measuring method and process are not rewritten to follow test results;\n     6\t> test results go into `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md`, and changing this page first needs Alex's word.\n     7\t> Amended 2026-11-04 (Alex replied \"go\" to the proposed amendment; D360): §5.2 drops \"also file under the other party's question\" and puts two\n     8\t> sentences in its place, one on choosing and one on splitting; §6's decision rule is rewritten. How it came about:\n     9\t> `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/prd-amendment-proposal.md` and `log.md` rows 11–16.\n    10\t> 2026-11-05 (D368): translated into English on Alex's word (\"不，用英文吧\"); §4 corrected to name the frozen standard's version 5 and §5 given a status line, on his delegation (\"你要来帮我看到底做不做\").\n    11\t\n    12\t## 0. Checklist before every start (Alex: \"每次开始前都需要revisit\")\n    13\t\n    14\tBefore starting any step, finish these in order; if one cannot be done, do not start:\n    15\t\n    16\t1. Read §§1–8 of this page in full; read the last three rows of `log.md`, and confirm where the previous step stopped and why.\n    17\t2. Check against §3, \"not in scope\": what this step does is not in that table. If it is in the table, stop and take it to Alex.\n    18\t3. Check against §4, \"constraints\": this step changes nothing frozen; a change to the frozen extraction prompt is the one place §5 names, and Alex's\n    19\t   word for it is already given.\n    20\t4. If this step costs money: its predictions and stop conditions are written down and frozen as §7 says (with their sha256 recorded beside them); the\n    21\t   requests are rebuilt byte for byte as §6 says; the control is re-sent the same day.\n    22\t5. What goes to Alex: one conclusion, one recommendation, one test, after §7's rounds of review; no menu of options; in plain words (the opening of\n    23\t   AGENTS, \"Explain and answer as to a sixteen-year-old\").\n    24\t\n    25\t## 1. Goal (end to end)\n    26\t\n    27\tEach question's judgement must receive the facts that can change its answer; facts that no question receives, those that bear on no question, must\n    28\tnot be piled into some one question. What is measured is the final judgement (the frozen judgement ruler, reader B, at the window end 2026-07-14), not\n    29\tany one step's own score.\n    30\t\n    31\t## 2. The core blocker (proven)\n    32\t\n    33\t**The system uses a fact for a question only when the fact \"states exactly this question's own figure\". An analyst also reasons onward from a fact: the other party's figure in a deal, price and volume for a product's revenue, a product's variants for its launch, and so on. The filing step cannot do this; the judging step reasons onward once it has the facts.**\n    34\t\n    35\tThis one blocker has two faces (described earlier as \"two problems\"):\n    36\t\n    37\t- **The parking lot.** Filing does not recognise facts that bear on a question indirectly, so no question takes them; yet to be kept, a fact must\n    38\t  first be filed (D209, D264). So D273 added the fallback sentence, which parks them in NVIDIA's total-revenue question. Of the reports under the\n    39\t  total-revenue question, in the part the gold read, 32–39% are ones the standard does not keep, 31–44% are kept but used by none of the 22 questions,\n    40\t  and only 7–10% are what the gold actually uses to judge total revenue (three draws); 24–44% of the total-revenue question's judgement citations are\n    41\t  news about other companies or the whole market (Intel graphics-card retail prices, a gas-fired power plant in Taiwan).\n    42\t- **Narrow questions starve.** Questions that only indirect facts can answer do not receive them: the custom-chip question received none of Broadcom's\n    43\t  figures in any of the three draws, and judged \"below $40 billion\" in all three; the operating-margin and NVIDIA capital-spending questions received\n    44\t  nothing at all and were never judged.\n    45\t\n    46\t**Evidence:**\n    47\t\n    48\t| Evidence | Content | Source |\n    49\t|---|---|---|\n    50\t| Filing recognises only \"its own figure\" | The model says \"Broadcom's AI chip revenue is not cloud providers' spending on custom chips\" and \"Google Cloud's backlog is not capital spending\"; on a neutral refiling (no question id named, the rule not shown), 23 of 42 facts that matter are still not filed | `probe-filing-account-d1.md`, `refile-no_fallback.json` |\n    51\t| The judgement reasons onward once it has the facts | The custom-chip question's request of 7-14, sent 3 times in each of the three draws: as it was, 9/9 \"below $40 billion\"; with the Broadcom, Trainium and other facts written in that draw's ledger added, 9/9 left the lowest rung (6 times $40–60 billion, 3 times $60–80 billion), reasoning as the two readers did (\"guidance $16 billion, about $64 billion annualised, less networking chips\") | `predictions-one-test.md`, `one-test-result.json`, `log.md` row 8 |\n    52\t| Where the parking lot came from | D273: with the fallback sentence added, the core items on the extraction ruler went 64 → 68; the extraction ruler does not look at which question a fact is filed under (AGENTS §7.1, \"What it does not measure\") | `docs/DECISIONS.md` D273 |\n    53\t| What the parking lot holds | The figures above; an independent reader on 12 random articles: of 25 misfilings as it was, 21 were parked in the revenue question; 8 and 5 with the fallback deleted | `sink_by_standard.py`, the independent reader's report (`log.md` row 8) |\n    54\t\n    55\tAll files above are in `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/` unless noted otherwise.\n    56\t\n    57\t## 3. Ruled out or secondary (outside this phase's scope, each recorded separately)\n    58\t\n    59\t| Matter | Conclusion | Source |\n    60\t|---|---|---|\n    61\t| The argument card | Not built; its code is deleted (Alex: \"好，删掉卡片代码\") | `docs/research/2026-11-04-argument-record/log.md` rows 4–11 |\n    62\t| The arrows of the question graph | Really wired backwards: a seed question's arrow means \"supports\" (D208), while the judging code reads it as \"whose conclusion to read\" (PRD 06). As a result themes are judged only after the total-revenue question, so the total-revenue question reads the themes' conclusions of the previous day, and in draw 3 a chain of questions went wrong in turn. But it does not explain the misfiling (filing comes before judging), and the gold itself judges data-center revenue as \"total revenue × 92%\". A topic of its own; settled by D368 (item 4: the arrow means \"supports\"), its fix to be built and measured | `core-problem.md`, `review-core-codex.md` |\n    63\t| Code removes the total-revenue question's own figures | True (\"keep only the narrowest question\" takes a seed arrow for \"a narrower sub-question\"), but each draw it costs the total-revenue question only 0–2 of the facts it needs | `log.md` row 8 |\n    64\t| A filing re-check added at the end | Recovers some, and misfiles about as many (independent reader: about one misfiled for each one filed right); not the solution | The independent reader's report |\n    65\t| One general sentence, \"the other party's figure in a deal counts too\" | The custom-chip question gains, but it pulls Morgan Stanley's price for a Rubin rack from the Rubin revenue question to the capital-spending question, and leaves some facts with no home | `answers.json` (the two arms definition and definition_no_fallback), `measure-corrected.txt` |\n    66\t| Tightening D259's \"leeway\" | Alex's own decision; this phase does not overturn it | D259 |\n    67\t| Opening questions (the Ohio guarantee was not opened); the two readers' disagreement on the China-share question | Another class of problem, not in this phase | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/diagnosis.md` §0 |\n    68\t| Model drift | With the same requests, today about half as many facts go into the total-revenue question as on 11-03; the cause is not proven. Compare only with a control re-sent the same day, never with the archive | `log.md` row 6 |\n    69\t\n    70\t## 4. Constraints (what must not move, and why)\n    71\t\n    72\t- **The frozen ruler** (AGENTS §7.1, D261, D262): the extraction standard, version 5 (the signed version 3 with D248's seven boundaries and D250's four\n    73\t  evidence boundaries and counting unit), golden-v5, the seed questions and the judgement gold; not a word changes; a disagreement is a gap in the\n    74\t  system (D263).\n    75\t- **The frozen extraction prompt** (D274): v74i (`poc-v108`) does not change by a word; what this phase changes is the one place that holds the filing\n    76\t  rule (the description of the `links` field), made into a new version with a new configuration and compared with v74i. Alex has given his word for this\n    77\t  work (2026-11-04: \"好你去解决问题\", \"开始做\"); making it the default needs his word again.\n    78\t- **Frozen opening and judging** (D354): this phase does not touch them.\n    79\t- **The standard's text** (in translation): §6, \"file under the narrowest open question; when no question takes it, record no home and drop it, and read\n    80\t  each one at evaluation\"; §5.1, \"makes the answer to some question more or less likely\", with four paths, \"demand, supply, competition, pricing\"; §5.3,\n    81\t  \"another company's matter is not kept when one cannot say which question about the covered company it moves through a path such as demand, supply,\n    82\t  competition or pricing\".\n    83\t- **Decisions**: D207 (one home per fact, the narrowest question), D209 (no home means dropped, each one read), D259 (leeway: a figure question takes\n    84\t  \"the demand, supply, competition and pricing that make it more or less likely\"; the total-revenue question taking more is an accepted cost, \"bounded by\n    85\t  the narrowest-question rule and the driver questions\"), D265 (code keeps only the narrowest), D273 (the fallback sentence), D355 (the two failed tests\n    86\t  of the previous filing topic).\n    87\t- **Prompt principles** (AGENTS §6): carry only the standard and add no new rule; a general hedge-fund analyst: every rule and example reads the same\n    88\t  for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip company, names neither NVIDIA nor its products or figures, and is not taken from a test article; a\n    89\t  classification is written as a closed set with two or three examples per class, replacing the abstract wording; quality is not fixed by lengthening\n    90\t  the prompt: remove as much as is added, or write down the reason.\n    91\t- **Code decides only \"is it the same thing\", never \"does it matter\"** (AGENTS §2).\n    92\t\n    93\t## 5. What to do\n    94\t\n    95\tStatus: the links-rule version that carried items 1–3 (v74l) failed its one test (§8 step 5); D361 keeps the fallback sentence, so item 3 is not carried out. The plan below is kept as written.\n    96\t\n    97\t1. **Replace the abstract sentence \"makes the answer more or less likely through demand, supply, competition or pricing\" with the few classes in which an analyst recognises an indirect relation, written as a closed set, each class with two or three examples from other industries** (AGENTS §6: one\n    98\t   abstract sentence is a coin flip on every send; examples replace it). A first draft of the classes (checked against the standard line by line at\n    99\t   design time; it holds only meanings the standard already has):\n   100\t   - The question's own figure: this question's figures, guidance, estimates and revisions (estimates for another period or another population follow\n   101\t     the existing rule).\n   102\t   - The other side of the same money: a seller's revenue from the buyers the question counts; those buyers' orders and backlog with these sellers.\n   103\t   - Price and volume: a product's price or shipments, for the question on that product's revenue.\n   104\t   - Variants and parts: a product's variants, alternatives, key parts and schedule changes, for the question on that product's launch.\n   105\t   - Demand, supply and competition moves with a number: customers' orders and capital-spending plans, suppliers' capacity and prices, competitors'\n   106\t     sales and share, for the question they affect.\n   107\t2. **State how to file a fact when two specific questions both bear on it** (the problem the `definition_no_fallback` arm exposed: Morgan Stanley's rack\n   108\t   price was filed under the buyers' capital-spending question, and the seller's Rubin revenue question lost it). One home per fact (D207): file it under\n   109\t   the question its figure measures most directly (for example, a hotel's room rate goes under its room-revenue question, not under the\n   110\t   travellers'-spending question; a parts maker's revenue from carmakers goes under the carmakers' parts-spending question, not under a rival parts\n     201 docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/FORECAST-UNAVAILABLE-DESIGN.md | tail -n +58; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r72d.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/FORECAST-UNAVAILABLE-DESIGN.md | tail -n +58; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r72d.md'","aggregated_output":"    58\t  - no theme pushes, no composite, no idea derived from its forecast.\n    59\t- **Ideas.** The release's idea composition (`idea.py`, `idea_composite.py`) runs on export data only. A number question enters it\n    60\t  as question and evidence with the marker. Its ladder is not read from storage in either the answer-format build or the summary\n    61\t  input (`idea_composite.py:255`, `:281`; `poc-v118-idea.json:94`). The test inspects the composed request itself, not only the\n    62\t  delivered card.\n    63\t- **The POC is unchanged.** Its API, MCP and page keep today's behaviour. Whether colleagues' POC page also changes is a separate\n    64\t  later choice for Alex.\n    65\t\n    66\t## 3. Identity: what a number question is (verified on four stores by council 72b)\n    67\t\n    68\tA view is a number question unless one of the following excludes it:\n    69\t1. **An event.** The view key's measure is in `identity.EVENT_MEASURES` (`event`, `policy_event`). That covers 85 of 85 event\n    70\t   questions in the three comparison stores.\n    71\t2. **A Yes/No question.** The ladder has exactly two labels which, canonicalised with the existing vocabulary\n    72\t   (`vocabulary.canonical`), are Yes and No in either order. The sealed ledger's 30 such questions use `是` and `否`. The Xbox `v37`\n    73\t   (`growth_rate`, bounds around zero) is Yes/No.\n    74\t\n    75\tEvery other view is a number question. Its forecast is withheld whether its bands come from `lo`/`hi`, parse from the conditions\n    76\t(the sealed `v3`), or cannot be resolved. An unresolved record is withheld and listed in the release check, with a key or without\n    77\t(council 72b: an `old-1/v8` copy without bounds does not parse, and its key still exists).\n    78\t\n    79\tCounts (council 72b), number / event or Yes/No:\n    80\t- `old-1` 19 / 28, `old-2` 17 / 26, `old-3` 18 / 31;\n    81\t- the sealed ledger: 43 parse as money, 21 are withheld as unresolved, 30 are Yes/No.\n    82\t\n    83\tNo wrong show-or-withhold result was found.\n    84\t\n    85\t**Events are binary too.** All 85 comparison-store events are also two-answer questions, including both known prose leaks, and a\n    86\tbinary event such as \"Restriction adopted\" / \"No restriction adopted\" exists in the served ledger (council 72c). §4's rule applies to\n    87\tevery question that is not a number question, events and Yes/No alike, and each keeps its answer label.\n    88\t\n    89\t## 4. Questions that are not number questions: an open choice for Alex (not decided here)\n    90\t\n    91\tD726 covers number questions. A Yes/No question's band is not a number forecast, but its stored explanation can quote one (§1). The\n    92\tallow-list must say which Yes/No fields the release shows:\n    93\t- **(a)** the question, its evidence and its answer (Yes/No and label), with no explanation text. Safe, and it removes the reasoning\n    94\t  readers would see.\n    95\t- **(b)** as (a), plus the explanation only for judgements whose request showed the judge no number question's state. Code can\n    96\t  decide this from the stored request (`views/judging.py`, which builds the related questions shown).\n    97\t- **Measured (council 72c),** matching each judgement to its archived request:\n    98\t  - (b) would keep explanations on 31 of 769, 44 of 776 and 43 of 958 judgements in the three comparison runs;\n    99\t  - for a reader at the latest answer, it would keep them on 21 of 85 questions;\n   100\t  - it would keep 73 of 73 in the sealed ledger.\n   101\t- **The limit of (b).** It cannot promise the prose quotes no number forecast. A request also carries the question's own previous\n   102\t  reasoning, and can carry another question's, either of which may repeat a forecast seen earlier. Both known leaks are in\n   103\t  `why_changed`.\n   104\t- **Recommendation: (a) for the first release** (council 72c; Claude agrees, withdrawing its lean for (b)). It is the only option\n   105\t  that guarantees no number forecast reaches a reader through prose. (b) remains a measured later step.\n   106\t\n   107\t## 5. Tests and the $0 release check (each names its row above)\n   108\t\n   109\t- **Fixtures (real store on a temporary path):**\n   110\t  - a number question with `lo`/`hi`;\n   111\t  - one with bounds only in its conditions;\n   112\t  - an unresolved one with a key;\n   113\t  - a Yes/No question with numeric bounds;\n   114\t  - an event question;\n   115\t  - a Yes/No judgement whose prose quotes a number band;\n   116\t  - a real closing switch;\n   117\t  - an idea.\n   118\t- **What the tests assert.** `release_export`'s output holds only allow-listed keys (a schema check that fails on any extra key). For\n   119\t  number questions it holds no band, ladder, reasons, history, date or closing band; Yes/No follows §4's choice. The static build\n   120\t  refuses foreign input, and a release page makes no network fetch.\n   121\t- **Mutants:**\n   122\t  - an extra field;\n   123\t  - a fetch left on;\n   124\t  - a ladder read in idea composition;\n   125\t  - the identity mis-sorting each fixture.\n   126\t\n   127\t  Each must fail.\n   128\t- **End-to-end acceptance (council 72c):**\n   129\t  - an idea whose only question is `old-1/v8`: the card keeps its sources and \"Forecast unavailable\", with no scored driver and no\n   130\t    substitute band (the current composite expects an endpoint for every scoring candidate, `idea_composite.py:214`, `:231`);\n   131\t  - in the same release package, the two known event answers keep their labels and carry no spending forecast under the chosen\n   132\t    prose rule.\n   133\t- **The $0 release check.**\n   134\t  - On `old-1/v8`, confirm the marker and the same sources in the page and in the export data.\n   135\t  - Exercise closure on a temporary copy with a real closing switch, after first confirming that the unrestricted response exposes the\n   136\t    final band there.\n   137\t  - Check ideas with a recorded or test transport, inspecting the composed request.\n   138\t  - Use `old-3/v36` and `old-1/v10` as the Yes/No prose cases.\n   139\t  - Use one unchanged event question as the control.\n   140\t\n   141\t## 6. Not in scope\n   142\t\n   143\t- The judging, opening and extraction pieces (frozen).\n   144\t- The closed v13s line.\n   145\t- Other open defects.\n   146\t- No prompt or pipeline change.\n   147\t- The POC's own behaviour.\n     1\t1. Revision 4 adds the package boundary and acceptance case that 72c requested. The builder must call the exporter directly, and the check covers the finished package. The `old-1/v8` idea card must show its sources and “Forecast unavailable,” with no scored driver or substitute band. The same package must preserve both event labels and omit their spending forecasts. I found no unrelated change. [Revision 3 snapshot:15](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r72c.stream:15) · [Design:37](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/FORECAST-UNAVAILABLE-DESIGN.md:37) · [Design:129](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/FORECAST-UNAVAILABLE-DESIGN.md:129)\n     2\t2. One prose rule now covers events and Yes/No questions, including all 85 overlapping cases. The figures match 72c. Option (b) would preserve explanations for 21 of 85 latest answers and 73 of 73 historical answers in the sealed ledger. The design recommends (a) to Alex. Under (a), I found no remaining route in the design for a stored number forecast to reach a release reader. [72c:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r72c.md:5) · [Design:85](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/FORECAST-UNAVAILABLE-DESIGN.md:85) · [Design:98](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/FORECAST-UNAVAILABLE-DESIGN.md:98)\n     3\t3. **One correction from 72c is incomplete.** Line 97 still tells Alex that an explanation kept under (b) “cannot quote a number forecast.” Line 102 correctly says it can. A Yes/No answer can repeat a spending forecast from its previous explanation even when the current request shows no number question directly. Delete the sentence spanning lines 96–97 that makes the guarantee. [Design:96](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/FORECAST-UNAVAILABLE-DESIGN.md:96) · [Design:102](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/FORECAST-UNAVAILABLE-DESIGN.md:102) · [judging.py:211](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:211)\n     4\t\n     5\t**Verdict — not ready as written.** The contradictory guarantee is the only blocker. Once removed, the design is ready to hand to release work when Alex opens it, with §4’s choice still his and **(a) recommended**.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^#+ .*D(726|756|757|758)'\"\\\\b|\"'^'\"\\\\| D(726|756|757|758)\\\\b' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; tail -n 30 docs/DECISIONS-log.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^#+ .*D(726|756|757|758)'\"\\\\b|\"'^'\"\\\\| D(726|756|757|758)\\\\b' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; tail -n 30 docs/DECISIONS-log.md\"","aggregated_output":"10991:## D726 · 2026-11-22 · Alex opens a judge version for related number questions ($0 gate first) and agrees to \"Forecast unavailable\" as the first-release interim\n11799:## D755 · 2026-11-23 · The paid re-send STOPs (complete); council 71: close v13s at STOP and carry D726's \"Forecast unavailable\" into the first release\n11823:## D756 · 2026-11-23 · Alex closes the v13s line; the first release shows number questions as \"Forecast unavailable\"\n11842:## D757 · 2026-11-23 · Toward production: the \"Forecast unavailable\" release design is ready for when Alex opens release work (council 72–72d); two choices go to Alex\n11867:## D758 · 2026-11-23 · Under D439 the council opens local release work for \"Forecast unavailable\"; non-number questions show no explanation text (§4 a); the v13s checker defects are closed as won't-fix (council 73)\n  - identity is structural: a question is a number question unless it is an event measure or a canonical Yes/No pair, and unresolved\n    records are withheld and listed. Verified on four stores with no wrong result;\n  - tests, mutants and an end-to-end acceptance on `old-1/v8` and the two known event leaks.\n  - **Why an allow-list.** Rounds 72 and 72b each found new leak paths in a reader-by-reader design, so revision 3 replaced that\n    approach with one structural rule.\n- **Open for Alex:**\n  1. whether to open release work now (then: build to this design, then the $0 release check);\n  2. what questions that are not number questions show in the release:\n     - (a) answer and label without explanation text, recommended by the council and Claude;\n     - (b) explanations only where the judge was shown no number question's state: 21 of 85 questions at the latest answer, and it\n       cannot guarantee that no number forecast is quoted.\n- **Not done.** Nothing is built and nothing is committed. The POC is unchanged.\n\n## D758 · 2026-11-23 · Under D439 the council opens local release work for \"Forecast unavailable\"; non-number questions show no explanation text (§4 a); the v13s checker defects are closed as won't-fix (council 73)\n\n- **Why the council decides.** Alex is asleep and delegated tonight's decisions (D750: \"这边的决策交给你，还有 Council … 准备要往\n  Production 方向去走了\"). He had already chosen the behaviour (D726 \"同意\"; D756 \"结束这条线，预测暂缺上线\"). D757 left two\n  choices for him; under D439 a choice that would go to Alex goes to the council when it is reversible and within the limits.\n- **Council round 73** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/round73-brief.md`, `codex-r73.md`, `claude-r73.md`), consensus:\n  1. **Release work opens tonight, local and at $0:** the exporter, the release builder, the package check, tests and mutants, the $0\n     release check. Nothing committed, pushed, published or deployed; the POC and colleagues' page unchanged. Reversible: the package\n     can be deleted without touching what the POC serves. D726's \"not started\" described the state on 2026-11-22 and set no gate.\n  2. **§4 (a):** an event or Yes/No question shows its question, evidence and answer with its original label, and no generated\n     explanation. It alone guarantees no number forecast reaches a reader through prose (option (b) cannot: a request carries the\n     question's earlier reasoning, `views/judging.py:211`). The cost is real: readers lose the written rationale for those answers.\n  3. **The v13s checker defects** (`PAID-V13S-RESULT.md` §5) are closed as won't-fix: `calc_small.py` has callers only in research\n     code, the line is closed (D756), and the STOP bars tuning on the 36 answers. The findings stay on record.\n  4. **Order:** the exporter and the checked package first; the reader's screen second (it completes D756's screen check). How the\n     idea path enters the release is settled in the build design's review (round 74).\n- **Still Alex's:** commit, push, publish, deploy, colleagues' POC page, any paid send or judge work.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
