 generate from stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027?', 'other')\nv38 ('Will NVIDIA complete an investment of roughly $2.5 billion to $3 billion in Thinking Machines Lab by 2027-09-04?', 'event')\nv15 (\"How much will NVIDIA's supply commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report?\", 'financing_amount')\nold-3 source values 1286 empty {'id': 0, 'title': 0, 'publisher': 0, 'uri': 0}\nserved source values 746 empty {'id': 0, 'title': 0, 'publisher': 0, 'uri': 0}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_47","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'from pathlib import Path\nimport sqlite3,collections,re,sys,json\nroot=Path(\\\"poc/data\\\")\nbase=root/\\\"comparison-2026-11-17\\\"\ndef conn(p):\n c=sqlite3.connect(p.resolve().as_uri()+\\\"?mode=ro&immutable=1\\\",uri=True)\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n return c\ninputs=[(f\\\"old-{i}\\\",base/f\\\"run/old-{i}/prod/views.sqlite\\\",base/\\\"items-comparison.sqlite\\\") for i in (1,2,3)]+[(\\\"served\\\",root/\\\"served-2026-10-28-v34-nvda-3m-through-0828/views.sqlite\\\",root/\\\"items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite\\\")]\nfor n,vid in [(1,\\\"v38\\\"),(2,\\\"v33\\\")]:\n name,p,ip=inputs[n];c=conn(p);i=conn(ip)\n r=c.execute(\\\"select question,created_from_input,created_seq from views where view_id=?\\\",(vid,)).fetchone(); a=i.execute(\\\"select title,publisher,text from items where input_id=?\\\",(r[1],)).fetchone()\n print(name,vid,tuple(r),a[0]); print(a[2][:1080])\n print(\\\"j first\\\",tuple(c.execute(\\\"select seq,implied_outcome from judgements where view_id=? order by judgement_id limit 1\\\",(vid,)).fetchone()))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_47","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'from pathlib import Path\nimport sqlite3,collections,re,sys,json\nroot=Path(\\\"poc/data\\\")\nbase=root/\\\"comparison-2026-11-17\\\"\ndef conn(p):\n c=sqlite3.connect(p.resolve().as_uri()+\\\"?mode=ro&immutable=1\\\",uri=True)\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n return c\ninputs=[(f\\\"old-{i}\\\",base/f\\\"run/old-{i}/prod/views.sqlite\\\",base/\\\"items-comparison.sqlite\\\") for i in (1,2,3)]+[(\\\"served\\\",root/\\\"served-2026-10-28-v34-nvda-3m-through-0828/views.sqlite\\\",root/\\\"items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite\\\")]\nfor n,vid in [(1,\\\"v38\\\"),(2,\\\"v33\\\")]:\n name,p,ip=inputs[n];c=conn(p);i=conn(ip)\n r=c.execute(\\\"select question,created_from_input,created_seq from views where view_id=?\\\",(vid,)).fetchone(); a=i.execute(\\\"select title,publisher,text from items where input_id=?\\\",(r[1],)).fetchone()\n print(name,vid,tuple(r),a[0]); print(a[2][:1080])\n print(\\\"j first\\\",tuple(c.execute(\\\"select seq,implied_outcome from judgements where view_id=? order by judgement_id limit 1\\\",(vid,)).fetchone()))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"old-2 v38 ('Will NVIDIA complete an investment of roughly $2.5 billion to $3 billion in Thinking Machines Lab by 2027-09-04?', 'gnews:21017fa01f34cb308af3b38b', 1394) Nvidia Is Eyeing a $2.5 Billion Bet on Mira Murati's AI Startup\nNvidia Corp. ( NVDA , Financials ), the dominant supplier of artificial intelligence chips, could put roughly $2.5 billion into Mira Murati's Thinking Machines Lab as it expands its reach beyond selling hardware.\nThinking Machines is in talks to raise between $5 billion and $6 billion at a valuation of at least $40 billion before the new investment, according to The Information.\nNvidia is expected to provide roughly half of that capital. The potential deal would deepen an already close relationship.\nIn March, Nvidia and Thinking Machines announced a multiyear partnership to deploy at least 1 gigawatt of next-generation Vera Rubin systems. The first deployment is targeted for early next year. That makes the investment strategically different from a typical venture bet.\nNvidia would be helping finance an AI company that has already committed to deploying its infrastructure at enormous scale. The arrangement could strengthen future demand for Nvidia systems while giving Thinking Machines access to the computing capacity needed to build and serve advanced AI models.\nT\nj first (1429, 'Rung 1 (Yes): if the reported $5–6 billion fundraising closes, NVIDIA’s expected roughly half would imply an investment of about $2.5–3 billion, within the question’s amount and due-date scope. The report does not specify the fundraising period or confirm completion, but the due date is one year away and the stated expectation makes completion by then the likelier outcome.')\nold-3 v33 ('At NVIDIA’s FY2027 annual filing, what amount of equity investment commitments will NVIDIA report as outstanding or committed?', 'gnews:d024d71d75661d369798d307', 821) Nvidia's $70 billion bet on OpenAI, Anthropic, and others could pay off big for shareholders\nArguably, no one has more at stake than Nvidia ( NVDA ) in the AI boom.\nNext-gen companies such as Elon Musk's SpaceX ( SPCX ) are clamoring for Nvidia's high-powered computing chips , which are at the heart of the investment thesis on the company.\nBut investors should also consider the formidable investment portfolio Nvidia now holds. When companies like OpenAI ( OPAI.PVT ) and Anthropic ( ANTH.PVT ) inevitably go public at stratospheric valuations within the next year, Nvidia's early backing of these companies stands to drive additional value for its shareholders.\nNvidia has committed to directly providing $70 billion in equity investments into ecosystem partners, per new research from Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya. Recent investments include $30 billion in OpenAI, up to $10 billion in Anthropic, and $5 billion in Safe Superintelligence.\n\"Overall, we view Nvidia's $70 billion investments as easily manageable given its ability to generate as much as about $470 billion of free cash flow over just the next two years (2026-2027), or about 15% of free cash flow,\nj first (834, 'About $70 billion of equity investment commitments, based on Arya’s estimate that NVIDIA has committed that amount to ecosystem partners; the estimate’s period is unspecified, but it is stated as commitments already made. That amount reaches the rung-5 boundary of $70 billion.')\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_48","type":"agent_message","text":"**Verdict: not ready to build.** The proposed check would accept Hivemind’s own forecast inside an allowed text field. It would also flag genuine source material. Both failures are reproducible on `old-1/v8`: the check misses the stored forecast of about **$390 billion**, while a source quote legitimately contains the withheld label **“Bullish.”** [Release check:197](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-BUILD.md:197) · [Evidence returned to the exporter:232](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/service.py:232)\n\n### 1. What can reach a reader\n\nI checked the three comparison stores and the served ledger read-only. I found **no confirmed case of a stored judgement being copied into an evidence record**. I did find a meaningful difference between the article, the system’s summary of it, and the system’s own answer. The build design treats all three as strings.\n\n| Stored case | What the reader would see |\n|---|---|\n| `old-1/v8/e2295` | Morningstar estimates **$361 billion**. Its quote gives that figure and its summary attributes it to Morningstar. This is precisely the evidence the release should preserve. |\n| `old-1/v15/e3238` | The quote gives **$20 billion** this year and **“more than double”** next year. The system’s one-line summary supplies **“more than $40 billion.”** That is faithful arithmetic, but the system wrote the dollar figure. |\n| `old-2/v38`, `e3877` | The article title gives **$2.5 billion**. The article says NVIDIA may provide roughly half of a **$5–6 billion** round. Both the stored *question* and the evidence summary spell out **$2.5–3 billion**. The question already contained the range before its first judgement. |\n| `old-1/v8/e5309` | The source title forecasts **$31.6 trillion** of global spending by 2050. The linked evidence concerns NVIDIA’s reported first-half revenue. The title is a publisher’s forecast and belongs with its source. |\n\nThe writer checks the quote against the article. It stores the generated `one_line` separately. Linking the two to a number question establishes neither that every amount was quoted nor that the summary contains a Hivemind judgement. Keep attributed source forecasts and the original question. For the release promise of “amounts as quoted,” use the source wording where the summary introduces a calculated figure. Verify each exported value against its designated origin. A valid key name alone cannot establish that origin. [Signed design:47](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/FORECAST-UNAVAILABLE-DESIGN.md:47) · [Quote check:671](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/extraction.py:671) · [Evidence writer:29](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/writing.py:29) · [Question writer:38](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/writing.py:38)\n\n### 2. Missing or wrong table entries\n\n| Table | What changes the build |\n|---|---|\n| State × event | **Missing source item.** `item_lookup` substitutes the input ID for the title and empty strings for publisher and URI. All pass the proposed string checks. Refuse a visible source that cannot be matched to its item. The four store/item pairs I checked had zero missing source IDs. [Lookup:28](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/bundle.py:28) · [Proposed value checks:62](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-BUILD.md:62) |\n| State × event | **Failure after rename.** The builder puts the directory in its final location before checking the package and writing the report. A failed check leaves a final-looking directory, and a retry refuses it. Check the staged package before rename. Specify what happens if only the report fails and how the user can retry. [Build order:67](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-BUILD.md:67) · [Crash and retry rows:100](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-BUILD.md:100) |\n| Value × reader | **The manifest is a second place to put a forecast.** The proposed package check verifies the data hash but leaves manifest keys and agreement on `format`, `allow_list` and `as_of` unspecified. An extra forecast in `manifest.json` leaves the data hash intact. Require exactly the four manifest fields and cross-check their values. Include the report and its unresolved-question reader in the table. [Package check:69](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-BUILD.md:69) · [Reader table:109](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-BUILD.md:109) |\n| New path × old checks | **Date validation was lost.** `resolve_bound` compares the supplied text to the last completed day. `bundle.library` separately rejects a missing default and parses the date. An impossible date such as `2026-08-99` sorts before the comparison stores’ September 20 bound. Carry over both checks. Distinguish a missing default from a valid date before the first visible question, which can yield an empty library. [Bound:64](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/service.py:64) · [Library:227](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/bundle.py:227) |\n| Value × reader and old checks | **The named helpers do more than the table says.** `state_as_of` reads all judgement columns and decodes theme pushes. `rungs_of` reads conditions and bounds. Use a narrow label/position read for the package if the “never read” promise stands. Also unpack `ladder_bands` in the report: failure returns **`(None, reason)`**. Testing the whole return value for `None` silently misses unresolved questions. [Build claims:125](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-BUILD.md:125) · [Judgement reader:129](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/service.py:129) · [Rung reader:97](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/store.py:97) · [Band result:499](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/service.py:499) |\n\nSection 7 also records bytes and seconds without a pass threshold. Set the size and speed budgets before the first control, as the repository requires. [Release check:209](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-BUILD.md:209) · [AGENTS.md:120](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:120)\n\n### 3. Tests and mutants\n\nThe tests exist only on paper. Several would still pass with the intended protection removed.\n\n- **Tests 1, 2 and 7** catch an extra key or a copied passage. Add a mutant that keeps every key valid and puts the number judgement into `one_line`. Exercise the exporter through the builder. Check the exact source-backed value and confirm a refused build produces no final package. Calling `check_export` on an edited dictionary alone does not exercise the writer’s refusal. [Tests:163](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-BUILD.md:163) · [Writer requirement:124](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:124)\n- **Tests 3, 4, 5, 6 and 11** need fixtures that distinguish the branches. Defaulting to `number` passes test 3 even if the unresolved report lists every number question. Assert a condition-only *resolved* question is absent from that report and an unresolved one is present, both with and without a key. For test 6 use the served ledger’s `v49` labels, **“Restriction adopted” / “No restriction adopted.”** All 85 comparison-store events also have Yes/No labels, so removing event recognition can leave the displayed answer unchanged. Exercise the legacy case with the key table genuinely absent. [Tests:168](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-BUILD.md:168) · [Event overlap:85](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/FORECAST-UNAVAILABLE-DESIGN.md:85) · [Legacy lookup:159](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/store.py:159)\n- **Tests 8–10** have useful controls for evidence dates, foreign files and changed bytes. Add an unchanged-data-hash mutant with extra manifest prose. For the two known event leaks, export at **August 25** for `old-3/v36/j799` and **August 28** for `old-1/v10/j865`. I verified those are the last judgements on those dates. The default September 20 exports select **j1698** and **j1581**, so absence of the older prose there proves little. Assert the original answer label as well as the absence of the forecast. [Tests:175](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-BUILD.md:175) · [Stored leak dates:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r72b.md:1)\n\n### 4. The $0 sweep\n\n**Reject the 12-character floor as a release gate.** On `old-1/v8` it skips three of five labels: `Bullish`, `Neutral` and `Bearish`. Removing the floor still gives a wrong result. `old-1/v8/e1865` genuinely begins its source quote with **“Bullish confirmation would require…”**. Section 7’s requirement that none of the five labels occur anywhere conflicts with preserving that quote. [Sweep and acceptance:197](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-BUILD.md:197)\n\nI also checked the opposite error in memory. The **entire** `old-1/v8/j1580.implied_outcome`, beginning “About $390 billion,” contains **zero** of §7’s searched strings of 12 or more characters. The restatement “Our estimate for the year is $390bn” also produces zero. The sweep omits `implied_outcome`, `reference`, numeric bounds and structured judgement values. Searching serialized JSON also changes how quotation marks and newlines appear. Use exact matches on **decoded fields** as diagnostics, including short labels. Make the pass condition an independent comparison of package fields with their permitted source values at the requested date. Preserve the genuine $390 billion article quote in `old-1/e3597` as the control. [Stored judgement fields:18](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/store.py:18) · [Proposed sweep:201](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-BUILD.md:201)\n\n### 5. Decisions on the four open points\n\n| Open point | Decision |\n|---|---|\n| Idea submission | **Defer it.** The static release should offer no submission control or route. Carry the `old-1/v8` idea-card and composed-request acceptance forward as pending for the first idea-enabled slice. D758 expressly delegates this sequencing decision. [D758:11881](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11881) · [Idea acceptance:128](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/FORECAST-UNAVAILABLE-DESIGN.md:128) |\n| `object` and `kind` | **Drop both from the first package.** D757 authorizes the question, its sources and the marker. The question ID already supplies identity. The proposed object grouping is a separate display choice. [D757:11850](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11850) · [Proposed additions:214](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-BUILD.md:214) |\n| Research groups | **Leave them out.** They add generated names, scopes and aliases, and the agreed release screen needs none of them. [Group reader:213](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/bundle.py:213) · [D756 screen requirement:11833](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11833) |\n| Sweep floor | **Remove it as a safety criterion.** Keep diagnostic hits attributable to a particular question, field and source. [Release check:197](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-BUILD.md:197) |\n\n**The decisive acceptance case** is one package that preserves Morningstar’s **$361 billion** estimate and the genuine **“Bullish confirmation”** quote, yet fails when Hivemind’s **$390 billion** answer is substituted into that same evidence field or added to the manifest. Revise the design around that case before coding."}}
{"type":"turn.completed","usage":{"input_tokens":1819882,"cached_input_tokens":1731200,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":10685,"reasoning_output_tokens":1563}}
